n: How close are we, really, to making cars see and think for themselves? It’s a critical topic whose complexities could fill a book — so we’ve produced one.\n\nWith a forward by Euro NCAP (The European New Car Assessment Programme) Secretary General Michiel van Ratingen, “AspenCore’s Guide to Sensors in Automotive” is available for purchase now at EE Times’ Book Store.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFR2284429B1 - - Google Patents (https://patents.google.com/patent/FR2284429B1/en)\nciteturn16search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 47.4 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n# FR2284429B1 - - Google Patents\n\n## Info\n\nPublication number\n    FR2284429B1\nFR2284429B1 FR7528155A FR7528155A FR2284429B1 FR 2284429 B1 FR2284429 B1 FR 2284429B1 FR 7528155 A FR7528155 A FR 7528155A FR 7528155 A FR7528155 A FR 7528155A FR 2284429 B1 FR2284429 B1 FR 2284429B1 Authority\n    FR\n    France\nLegal status (The legal status is an assumption and is not a legal conclusion. Google has not performed a legal analysis and makes no representation as to the accuracy of the status listed.)\n    Expired\nApplication number\n    FR7528155A\nOther languages\n    French (fr)\nOther versions\n    FR2284429A1 (en\nCurrent Assignee (The listed assignees may be inaccurate. Google has not performed a legal analysis and makes no representation or warranty as to the accuracy of the list.)\n    Imperial Chemical Industries Ltd\nOriginal Assignee\n    Imperial Chemical Industries Ltd\nPriority date (The priority date is an assumption and is not a legal conclusion. Google has not performed a legal analysis and makes no representation as to the accuracy of the date listed.)\n    1974-09-13\nFiling date\n    1975-09-12\nPublication date\n    1979-06-22\n    1975-09-12 Application filed by Imperial Chemical Industries Ltd filed Critical Imperial Chemical Industries Ltd\n    1976-04-09 Publication of FR2284429A1 publication Critical patent/FR2284429A1/en\n    1979-06-22 Application granted granted Critical\n    1979-06-22 Publication of FR2284429B1 publication Critical patent/FR2284429B1/fr\n    Status Granted legal-status Critical Current\n\n## Classifications\n\n  *     * B—PERFORMING OPERATIONS; TRANSPORTING\n    * B32—LAYERED PRODUCTS\n    * B32B—LAYERED PRODUCTS, i.e. PRODUCTS BUILT-UP OF STRATA OF FLAT OR NON-FLAT, e.g. CELLULAR OR HONEYCOMB, FORM\n    * B32B38/00—Ancillary operations in connection with laminating processes\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMaharashtra State Gazetteers - Greater Bombay District (https://gazetteers.maharashtra.gov.in/cultural.maharashtra.gov.in/english/gazetteer/greater_bombay/industries.html)\nciteturn16search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 12.4 years ago; \n\n1.Manufacture of industrial organic and inorganic chemicals and goods such as acids, alkalies and their salts,          gases like acetylene, oxygen, nitrogen, etc.\n2.  Manufacture of fertilisers and pesticides.\n3.  Manufacture of paints, varnishes and lacquers.\n4.  Manufacture of drugs and medicines.\n5. Manufacture of perfumes, cosmetics, lotions, hair dressing, tooth paste, soaps in various forms such as synthetic,  detergent, shampoos, shaving products, cleaners, washing and scouring products and other toilet preparations.\n6.  Manufacture of turpentine, synthetic resins, plastic materials and synthetic fibres like nylons, teryelene except     glass.\n7. Manufacture of chemical products not elsewhere classified (including photo-chemicals, sensitised films and        papers).\n\nThe A.S.I, statistics for the chemicals and chemical products industry and its seven various sectors for the survey periods of 1973-74 and 1975-77 which are given in Table No. 10 are self-evident and need no particular comments. It may be clarified that the statistics for the survey periods are annual averages within the periods.\n\nIt may be very interesting to analyse the principal characteristics of the chemicals and chemical products industry in Bombay on the basis of the Annual Survey of Industries of 1975-77.\n\nThe chemicals and chemical products industry in Bombay comprised 454 registered factories which formed 49.02 per cent of those in Maharashtra in 1975-77 survey period. They provided employment to 53,479 persons which constituted 51.24 per cent of the employment in the industry in State. The invested capital in the factories in Bombay was to the tune of Rs. 4,09,17 lakhs or 47.20 per cent of that in Maharashtra. It is noteworthy that the capital investment in this industry in Bombay was about 135 per cent of that in the cotton textile industry, and was very much higher than that in any of the seven major sectors of industry in Bombay. This brings home the fact that it is a highly capital intensive industry requiring very sophisticated machinery.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFEBRUARY 2021 (https://www.eetimes.com/?download_id=1465760&smd_process_download=1)\nciteturn16search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; \nAutonocast mobility technology podcast.\nAutomated-vehicle technology is \nextremely complex, making it an intrinsi-\ncally challenging and intimidating topic for \neducators and the media. Compounding this \nchallenge is the lack of credible, independent \nreference material from which educators can \ndraw trustworthy information.\nWith “AspenCore Guide to Sensors in \nAutomotive: Making Cars See and Think \nAhead,” AspenCore global editor-in-chief \nJunko Yoshida and her top-notch band of \ncollaborators have stepped up to fill this gap \nin one of the most important aspects of the \ntechnology. Leveraging the impeccable cred-\nibility, deep connections in the industry, and \nknack for explaining challenging technical \ntopics that she is known for, Yoshida has \nbrought together an indispensable resource \nfor anyone in need of a one-stop education \non automotive sensors.\nThough technology changes fast, this book \nnot only provides an overview at a pivotal \nmoment, when autonomous-vehicle devel-\nopment appears to be maturing into actual \ndriverless services, but explains the trends \nthat are shaping that change as well.\nCritically for a book in this space, it goes \nbeyond just explaining individual technol-\nogies and provides the broader context of \nhow they work within automated driving \nsystems, how they complement and compare \nwith each other, and why their strengths and \nweaknesses matter.\nThis context, which explains percep-\ntion and automated driving systems more \nbroadly, will help “AspenCore Guide to \n“AspenCore Guide to Sensors in Automotive: \nMaking Cars See and Think Ahead” is  \navailable now at the EE Times bookstore.  \nVisit www.eetimes.com/shop for more  \ninformation and to purchase. \nFEBRUARY 2021 | www.eetimes.eu--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAmide derivatives Patent Grant Holloway , et al. December 11, 1 [Imperial Chemical Industries] (https://uspto.report/patent/grant/4977148)\nciteturn16search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 35.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; \n\nInventors:  | Holloway; Brian R. (Congleton, GB2), Howe; Ralph (MacClesfield, GB2), Rao; Balbir S. (Holmes Chapel, GB2), Stribling; Donald (Prestbury, GB2)\nAssignee:  | Imperial Chemical Industries (London, GB2)\nFamily ID:  | 26291076\nAppl. No.:  | 07/490,406\nFiled:  | March 8, 1990\n\nRelated U.S. Patent Documents\n\n* * *\n\n |  |  |  |  | \n | Application Number  | Filing Date  | Patent Number  | Issue Date  | \n | 213259  | Jun 29, 1988  | 4927836  |  | \n | 75983  | Jul 21, 1987  |  |  | \n\n* * *\n\nForeign Application Priority Data\n\n* * *\n\n |  |  |  | \nJul 23, 1986 [GB]  |  |  | 8617986\nJan 28, 1987 [GB]  |  |  | 8701832\nCurrent U.S. Class:  | 514/183; 514/416; 514/489; 514/522; 540/467; 540/476; 540/546; 544/58.4; 544/163; 514/227.5; 514/237.5; 514/311; 514/415; 514/